## Why This Is Asked
Tests reliability of composite APIs with dependent services.

## Key Concepts
- Downstream latency, timeouts, partial failures, circuit breakers
- Idempotent retries, exponential backoff, observability
- End-to-end timing and schema validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test using jest + nock
const nock = require('nock');
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../app');
test('partial data when one downstream times out', async () => {
  nock('http://sales').get('/daily').delay(3000).reply(200, {sales: 1000});
  nock('http://customers').get('/daily').reply(200, {customers: 50});
  const res = await request(app).get('/reports/daily');
  expect(res.statusCode).toBe(200);
  expect(res.body).toHaveProperty('sales');
  expect(res.headers).toHaveProperty('x-downstream-warn');
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test circuit-breaker behavior under sustained latency?
- What metrics would you validate in your observability stack?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests distributed systems correctness under concurrency, idempotency, ordering, and partial outages; validates that the API and ledger service maintain exactly-once semantics and message ordering.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys, exactly-once processing
- Ordering guarantees in distributed ledger
- Partial upstream outages and retry semantics
- End-to-end testing with simulated upstream services and timeouts
- Tooling: Postman/Newman, k6, WireMock, Kafka test doubles

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example k6 test that repeats a payment request with same idempotency key
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';

export default function () {
  const payload = { amount: 1000, currency: 'USD', account: 'A1' };
  const headers = { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Idempotency-Key': 'abc-123' };
  const res = http.post('https://api/payments', JSON.stringify(payload), { headers });
  check(res, { 'status 200': (r) => r.status === 200 || r.status === 409 });

  sleep(0.5);
}
```

Note: The code snippet is minimal; ensure you adapt to your API.

## Why This Is Asked
Advanced GraphQL testing in multi-tenant SaaS is complex due to caching, ACLs, and nested resolvers. This question probes the ability to design end-to-end tests that catch N+1, improper isolation, and stale caches during deployments.

## Key Concepts
- GraphQL persisted queries and hash-based routing
- DataLoader-like batching to prevent N+1
- Depth/complexity controls and per-tenant ACLs
- Cache invalidation across tenants and schema rollouts

## Code Example
```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check } from 'k6';
export const options = { vus: 100, duration: '30s' };
export default function () {
  const body = JSON.stringify({
    extensions: {"persistedQuery": {"version": 1, "sha256Hash": "abcd1234"}},
    variables: {"tenantId": "t1", "id": "u123"}
  });
  const res = http.post('https://api.example.com/graphql', body, {
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }
  });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
}
```

